---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
const body = "<p><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_cover.webp\" alt=\"Passed Once. Failed Forever.\" loading=\"eager\"></p><p>New York, 1854. At the Crystal Palace, Elisha Otis stands on a hoisting platform high above the crowd and has the only rope cut.</p><p>The platform drops a few inches and stops.</p><p>&quot;All safe, gentlemen. All safe.&quot;</p><p>The crowd loved the demo. But a building does not need the brake to work once, in front of a crowd. It needs it to work on every ride, for decades. It took until 1921 for the industry to write that down: ASME&#x27;s first US elevator safety code, 25 pages long.</p><p>An agent demo is the Crystal Palace. Production is every ride.</p><p>So I built a simulated agent, gave it 200 tasks, ran every task 20 times and counted the same results three ways.</p><p>Every one of my 200 tasks passed at least once. 178 of them also failed at least once.</p><p>pass@1, one run: 80%.<br>pass@5, any of five runs: 99.9%.<br>pass^5, read pass hat five, all five runs: 39%.<br>Five tools in a row, all five runs: 14%.</p><p>The demo number and the customer number come from the same runs.<br>Only one of them is the truth your customer feels.</p><p>This is why it matters now. Stephan Rabanser, Sayash Kapoor, Arvind Narayanan and colleagues at Princeton tracked 15 models over 24 months of releases. Accuracy rose. Reliability barely moved.</p><p>The fix comes from benchmark designers. \u03c4-bench introduced pass^k in 2024 to measure whether an agent gets the same task right every time. This October, Felix Chen, Stephan Rabanser and colleagues at Princeton put it plainly in their survey of agent evaluation: pass@k should not serve as evidence of reliability.</p><h2>Same problem, different pipe</h2><p>Otis proved a brake could work. The elevator code proved every brake, door lock and speed limit worked, on every ride. A demo shows that a run can pass. Reliability shows that runs keep passing.</p><p>My tasks need 1, 2, 3 or 5 tool calls in a row, and every tool call works between 85% and 99% of the time, like real tools. The runs are seeded, so every laptop gets the same numbers.</p><h2>Move one. Count every ride, not the demo</h2><p>One cut rope at a fair proved nothing about the ten thousandth ride.</p><p>I run every task 20 times and count passes. From those 20 runs I estimate three numbers. pass@1 is the chance one run works. pass@k is the chance at least one of k runs works. pass^k is the chance all k runs work.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def pass_at_k(n: int, c: int, k: int) -&gt; float:      # at least one of k works\n    return 1.0 if n - c &lt; k else 1 - comb(n - c, k) / comb(n, k)\n\ndef pass_hat_k(n: int, c: int, k: int) -&gt; float:     # all k work\n    return comb(c, k) / comb(n, k)</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step1_output.webp\" alt=\"Three tasks, the same 20 runs, counted three ways.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Three tasks, the same 20 runs, counted three ways.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show runs\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 pass_k.py --show runs</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>Both are the standard unbiased estimators: choose k runs out of the n you made. Task L3-01 passed 14 of 20 runs. Any of five, 99.96%. One run, 70%. All five, 14 choose 5 over 20 choose 5: 13%.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step1_data.webp\" alt=\"One task can be 99.96% and 13% at the same time.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>One task can be 99.96% and 13% at the same time.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: L3-01, pass@5 99.96%, pass^5 13%.</p><h2>Move two. Check every part of the machine</h2><p>The 1921 code covered switches, door locks and speed, not just the brake Otis made famous. A lift is only as safe as its weakest part.</p><p>An agent task is only as reliable as its weakest tool call. A run passes only if every call in the chain passes.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def run_once(task: dict) -&gt; bool:\n    return all(RNG.random() &lt; p for p in task[&quot;step_p&quot;])</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step2_output.webp\" alt=\"Longer chains fall faster, and repeats multiply the fall.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Longer chains fall faster, and repeats multiply the fall.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show chain\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 pass_k.py --show chain</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>Five calls that each work about 92% of the time pass 0.92 to the power 5, about 66%, and my five tool tasks pass 67% of single runs. Five runs in a row of a one tool task that passes 92% is roughly the same multiplication, and my table also says 66%. Chain length and repeats compound the same way, and together they compound twice: five tools, five runs, 14%.</p><p>A public benchmark of agents on live MCP servers found the same direction in single runs this year, and a much steeper fall: a solve rate of about 39% for tasks with 1 to 2 tools, down to 13% with 5 or more, a drop of two thirds. My single runs drop by about a quarter, 92% to 67%. Real tool chains are harsher than my simulation, and repeats make any fall steeper.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step2_data.webp\" alt=\"My chain lengths next to the public benchmark.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My chain lengths next to the public benchmark.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: 1 tool, pass@1 92% and pass^5 66%. 5 tools, pass@1 67% and pass^5 14%.</p><h2>Move three. Write the bar down</h2><p>The first elevator code was 25 pages. Today&#x27;s is over 350. Every page is a bar someone wrote down so that nobody had to argue about it on the day.</p><p>My bar is one line: pass^3 of 70% or more, or it does not ship.</p><pre><code class=\"language-python\">RELEASE_BAR = 0.70\n\ndef release_verdict(pass_hat_3: float) -&gt; str:\n    return &quot;SHIP&quot; if pass_hat_3 &gt;= RELEASE_BAR else &quot;DO NOT SHIP&quot;</code></pre><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step3_output.webp\" alt=\"The same agent, held to the bar a customer feels.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The same agent, held to the bar a customer feels.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"--show bar\"><button>Run this step in your browser</button><code>python3 pass_k.py --show bar</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><p>At pass@5 this agent would ship with a near perfect 99.9%. At pass^3 it scores 54% and does not ship. Nothing in the agent changed. Only the question did.</p><p>The interval line is there so nobody can argue it was bad luck. I resample the 200 tasks, and inside each task its 20 runs, 1,000 times. Resampling runs pushes pass^3 up a little, so I use the basic bootstrap, which reflects that push back around the 54% I measured. The 95% interval is 48% to 56%. Even the top is far below 70%.</p><p>One honest limit. This is a simulation, so it proves the arithmetic of the estimators, not the reliability of any real agent. My runs fail independently. Real failures cluster: the same hard task fails for the same reason again and again, which usually pushes pass^5 up toward pass@1. Tasks that are all equally hard push it down, toward 0.8 to the power 5, 33%. The only way to know where your agent lands is to run it. For a failure that moves money or touches safety, a bar of 70% is far too low; the bar there is close to zero failures.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_step3_data.webp\" alt=\"Four ways to report one agent, and the one that decides.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Four ways to report one agent, and the one that decides.</figcaption></figure><p>Data point: pass^3 54%, 95% interval 48% to 56%, bar 70%. Do not ship.</p><h2>The five ghosts, in both worlds</h2><p>The fairground demo. One cut rope, one cheering crowd. One green run in a meeting. Report pass^k, never a single run.</p><p>The best of five. Testing until the brake holds once. Rerunning a flaky eval until it passes. Count every run, including the bad ones.</p><p>The weakest part. A strong brake on a car with a broken door lock. A strong model with one flaky tool. Measure by chain length, not just in total.</p><p>The unwritten bar. Safety argued case by case. Release decided by whoever is most confident in the room. Write the pass^k bar in CI before the first run.</p><p>The quiet decline. A lift that slowly wears. An agent whose tools change underneath it. Rerun pass^k on every release and every tool upgrade.</p><h2>What I want you to steal</h2><p>I attached my working files to this edition.</p><p>You are on it. Every step below has a button that runs the real script in your browser, with Python loaded on the page. The same code is in the repo for your laptop.</p><p>Try it on your laptop. The code is in <a href=\"https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage/tree/main/editions/tl-19-pass-k\">editions/tl-19-pass-k</a>, standard library only.</p><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git clone https://github.com/anupsahoo/multi-agent-ticket-triage\ncd multi-agent-ticket-triage/editions/tl-19-pass-k &amp;&amp; python3 pass_k.py</code></pre><p>On Windows, type python instead of python3. The first line you should see is the table header: tools in chain, pass@1, pass@5, pass^3, pass^5. Each move above also runs on its own with --show runs, --show chain or --show bar.</p><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_run_output.webp\" alt=\"The full run: 200 tasks, 20 runs each, by chain length.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The full run: 200 tasks, 20 runs each, by chain length.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tl-run\" data-args=\"\"><button>Run the full script in your browser</button><code>python3 pass_k.py</code><pre class=\"tl-out\" hidden></pre></div><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_datasheet.webp\" alt=\"My log: every chain length and sample tasks you can check by hand.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My log: every chain length and sample tasks you can check by hand.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_scribble_note.webp\" alt=\"The demo line on top, and what customers feel underneath.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The demo line on top, and what customers feel underneath.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"/trust-layer/19/TL_19_handwritten_note.webp\" alt=\"The estimators worked out on paper.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The estimators worked out on paper.</figcaption></figure><p>The demo proves a run can pass. Only pass^k tells you it keeps passing.</p><p>A question for the leaders reading.</p><p>My agent scored 80% on one run and 39% on five in a row. What does your agent score when it has to pass five times in a row, and is that the number in your release decision?</p><p>I am collecting answers, and I read every reply.</p>";
const files = {"pass_k.py": "\"\"\"The Trust Layer, edition 19: Passed Once. Failed Forever.\n\n200 agent tasks that need 1, 2, 3 or 5 tool calls in a row. Every tool call\nsucceeds most of the time, like a real one. Each task is run 20 times, and the\nsame results are counted three ways:\n\n  pass@1  the chance a single run works\n  pass@k  the chance at least one of k runs works\n  pass^k  the chance all k runs work (what a customer who comes back feels)\n\nStandard library only. Seeded, so every machine gets the same numbers.\nRun:  python pass_k.py\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport csv\nimport json\nimport random\nimport sys\nfrom math import comb\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nHERE = Path(__file__).parent\nRNG = random.Random(19)\nN_RUNS = 20                       # runs per task\nCHAIN_LENGTHS = (1, 2, 3, 5)      # tool calls a task needs, in a row\nTASKS_PER_LENGTH = 50\n\n# 1. The tasks: each tool call has its own success rate, between 85% and 99% ----\ntasks = [{\"id\": f\"L{n}-{i:02}\", \"length\": n,\n          \"step_p\": [round(RNG.uniform(0.85, 0.99), 3) for _ in range(n)]}\n         for n in CHAIN_LENGTHS for i in range(1, TASKS_PER_LENGTH + 1)]\n\n\n# 2. One run: the task passes only if every tool call in the chain passes -------\ndef run_once(task: dict) -> bool:\n    return all(RNG.random() < p for p in task[\"step_p\"])\n\n\n# 3. The three ways to count c passes out of n runs (unbiased estimators) -------\ndef pass_at_k(n: int, c: int, k: int) -> float:      # at least one of k works\n    return 1.0 if n - c < k else 1 - comb(n - c, k) / comb(n, k)\n\n\ndef pass_hat_k(n: int, c: int, k: int) -> float:     # all k work\n    return comb(c, k) / comb(n, k)\n\n\n# 4. The release bar, written down\nRELEASE_BAR = 0.70                # pass^3 a release must reach\n\n\ndef release_verdict(pass_hat_3: float) -> str:\n    return \"SHIP\" if pass_hat_3 >= RELEASE_BAR else \"DO NOT SHIP\"\n\n\ndef interval(passes: list[int], k: int = 3, n_boot: int = 1000, seed: int = 191) -> tuple[float, float]:\n    \"\"\"Bootstrap 95% interval for mean pass^k. Resample the tasks, and inside each task\n    resample its 20 runs, so both task choice and run to run noise are covered.\n    1,000 resamples, basic (reflected) bootstrap interval.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    means = []\n    for _ in range(n_boot):\n        total = 0.0\n        for _ in passes:\n            c = rng.choice(passes)\n            c_star = sum(rng.random() < c / N_RUNS for _ in range(N_RUNS))\n            total += pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c_star, k)\n        means.append(total / len(passes))\n    means.sort()\n    point = sum(pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c, k) for c in passes) / len(passes)\n    # basic bootstrap: reflect around the estimate, which removes the upward pull of resampling runs\n    return 2 * point - means[int(0.975 * n_boot) - 1], 2 * point - means[int(0.025 * n_boot)]\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    rows = []\n    for t in tasks:\n        c = sum(run_once(t) for _ in range(N_RUNS))\n        true_p = 1.0\n        for p in t[\"step_p\"]:\n            true_p *= p\n        rows.append({\"task\": t[\"id\"], \"length\": t[\"length\"], \"passes\": c,\n                     \"true_p\": round(true_p, 3),\n                     \"pass@1\": round(pass_at_k(N_RUNS, c, 1), 3),\n                     \"pass@5\": round(pass_at_k(N_RUNS, c, 5), 3),\n                     \"pass^3\": round(pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c, 3), 3),\n                     \"pass^5\": round(pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c, 5), 3)})\n\n    def mean(key, group):\n        return sum(r[key] for r in group) / len(group)\n\n    print(f\"{'tools in chain':<16}{'pass@1':>8}{'pass@5':>8}{'pass^3':>8}{'pass^5':>8}{'tasks never failed':>21}\")\n    summary = {}\n    for n in CHAIN_LENGTHS + (\"all\",):\n        g = rows if n == \"all\" else [r for r in rows if r[\"length\"] == n]\n        clean = sum(r[\"passes\"] == N_RUNS for r in g)\n        summary[n] = {k: mean(k, g) for k in (\"pass@1\", \"pass@5\", \"pass^3\", \"pass^5\")}\n        s = summary[n]\n        print(f\"{str(n):<16}{s['pass@1']:>8.0%}{s['pass@5']:>8.1%}{s['pass^3']:>8.0%}{s['pass^5']:>8.0%}\"\n              f\"{f'{clean}/{len(g)}':>21}\")\n\n    with open(HERE / \"results.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n        w = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=list(rows[0]))\n        w.writeheader()\n        w.writerows(rows)\n\n    published = HERE / \"published.json\"       # the numbers printed in the article\n    if \"--publish\" in sys.argv:\n        published.write_text(json.dumps({str(k): v for k, v in summary.items()}, indent=1), encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    if \"--check\" in sys.argv:    # CI floor: the article's numbers must still come out of the code\n        want = json.loads(published.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n        return 0 if all(abs(want[str(n)][k] - v) < 1e-9 for n, s in summary.items() for k, v in s.items()) else 1\n    return 0\n\n\n# 5. One move at a time: python pass_k.py --show runs | chain | bar\ndef show(mode: str) -> int:\n    rng_state = RNG.getstate()\n    results = {t[\"id\"]: sum(run_once(t) for _ in range(N_RUNS)) for t in tasks}\n    RNG.setstate(rng_state)\n    if mode == \"runs\":\n        for tid in (\"L1-01\", \"L3-01\", \"L5-02\"):\n            c = results[tid]\n            print(f\"{tid}: passed {c} of {N_RUNS} runs   pass@1 {pass_at_k(N_RUNS, c, 1):.0%}   \"\n                  f\"pass@5 {pass_at_k(N_RUNS, c, 5):.2%}   pass^5 {pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c, 5):.0%}\")\n        return 0\n    groups = {n: [t for t in tasks if t[\"length\"] == n] for n in CHAIN_LENGTHS}\n    if mode == \"chain\":\n        for n, g in groups.items():\n            p1 = sum(pass_at_k(N_RUNS, results[t[\"id\"]], 1) for t in g) / len(g)\n            p5 = sum(pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, results[t[\"id\"]], 5) for t in g) / len(g)\n            print(f\"{n} tool{'s' if n > 1 else ' '} in a row   pass@1 {p1:.0%}   pass^5 {p5:.0%}\")\n        return 0\n    c_all = [results[t[\"id\"]] for t in tasks]\n    p3 = sum(pass_hat_k(N_RUNS, c, 3) for c in c_all) / len(c_all)\n    print(f\"pass@1 {sum(c / N_RUNS for c in c_all) / len(c_all):.0%}   \"\n          f\"pass@5 {sum(pass_at_k(N_RUNS, c, 5) for c in c_all) / len(c_all):.1%}   pass^3 {p3:.0%}\")\n    lo, hi = interval(c_all)\n    print(f\"pass^3 95% interval {lo:.0%} to {hi:.0%}\")\n    print(f\"release bar pass^3 >= {RELEASE_BAR:.0%}: {release_verdict(hi)} even at the top of the interval\" if hi < RELEASE_BAR\n          else f\"release bar pass^3 >= {RELEASE_BAR:.0%}: {release_verdict(p3)}\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    if \"--show\" in sys.argv:\n        sys.exit(show(sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--show\") + 1]))\n    sys.exit(main())\n", "published.json": "{\n \"1\": {\n  \"pass@1\": 0.92,\n  \"pass@5\": 1.0,\n  \"pass^3\": 0.77972,\n  \"pass^5\": 0.66276\n },\n \"2\": {\n  \"pass@1\": 0.851,\n  \"pass@5\": 0.99956,\n  \"pass^3\": 0.63512,\n  \"pass^5\": 0.49195999999999995\n },\n \"3\": {\n  \"pass@1\": 0.752,\n  \"pass@5\": 0.99878,\n  \"pass^3\": 0.436,\n  \"pass^5\": 0.26298\n },\n \"5\": {\n  \"pass@1\": 0.67,\n  \"pass@5\": 0.99586,\n  \"pass^3\": 0.30232,\n  \"pass^5\": 0.13699999999999998\n },\n \"all\": {\n  \"pass@1\": 0.79825,\n  \"pass@5\": 0.99855,\n  \"pass^3\": 0.53829,\n  \"pass^5\": 0.388675\n }\n}"};
---
<ArticleLayout title={"Passed Once. Failed Forever."} description={"80% on one run. 99.9% on any of five. 39% when all five had to work."} category="Newsletter"
  date="The Trust Layer · Issue 19" readTime="7 min read" tags={["AgentEvals", "MCP", "Agent Evals", "The Trust Layer"]}>
  <div set:html={body} />
  <script type="application/json" id="tl-files" set:html={JSON.stringify(files)} />
  <script is:inline data-script={"pass_k.py"}>
  (function () {
    const script = document.currentScript.dataset.script;
    let ready = null;
    function load() {
      if (ready) return ready;
      ready = new Promise((ok, bad) => {
        const s = document.createElement("script");
        s.src = "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/pyodide/v0.26.4/full/pyodide.js";
        s.onload = async () => {
          const py = await loadPyodide();
          const files = JSON.parse(document.getElementById("tl-files").textContent);
          py.FS.mkdirTree("/tl"); py.FS.chdir("/tl");
          for (const [name, text] of Object.entries(files)) py.FS.writeFile("/tl/" + name, text);
          ok(py);
        };
        s.onerror = bad; document.head.appendChild(s);
      });
      return ready;
    }
    document.querySelectorAll(".tl-run button").forEach(btn => btn.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const box = btn.parentElement, out = box.querySelector(".tl-out");
      out.hidden = false; btn.disabled = true;
      out.textContent = "Loading Python in your browser. The first run takes a few seconds.";
      try {
        const py = await load();
        out.textContent = "Running...";
        const args = JSON.stringify([script].concat(box.dataset.args ? box.dataset.args.split(" ") : []));
        const res = py.runPython(`
import io, sys, runpy, contextlib
buf = io.StringIO()
sys.argv = ${args}
code = 0
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buf):
    try:
        runpy.run_path("/tl/pass_k.py", run_name="__main__")
    except SystemExit as e:
        code = e.code or 0
buf.getvalue()`);
        out.textContent = res;
      } catch (e) { out.textContent = "Could not run here: " + e; }
      btn.disabled = false;
    }));
  })();
  </script>
</ArticleLayout>
